/-!
# Quasi-coherent modules and affines: the affine big sites are equivalent ringed topoi

Let `S` be a scheme inside a chosen big site and let `τ` be one of the Zariski, étale, smooth,
syntomic or fppf topologies. The affine objects over `S` form a dense subsite of the big
`τ`-site over `S`, so restriction to them is an equivalence of sheaf topoi; equipping the affine
subsite with the restriction of the structure sheaf `T/S ↦ Γ(T, 𝒪_T)` upgrades this to an
equivalence of *ringed* topoi

  `(Sh((Aff/S)_τ), 𝒪_Aff) ⟶ (Sh((Sch/S)_τ), 𝒪)`,

whose structure morphism is an isomorphism — indeed the identity on underlying sheaves of sets.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.commStructureSheaf`: the sheaf of commutative rings of global functions on
  a site of schemes over `S`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.uliftRingSheaf`: a sheaf of commutative rings with its sections lifted into
  a larger universe.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.affineToBigToposHom`: the morphism of topoi induced by a dense subsite
  inclusion, whose inverse image is restriction.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.affineToBigRingedToposHom`: the same morphism of topoi together with the
  structure morphism identifying the inverse image of the structure sheaf with its restriction.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.zariski_affineToBigRingedToposEquivalence`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.etale_affineToBigRingedToposEquivalence`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.smooth_affineToBigRingedToposEquivalence`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.syntomic_affineToBigRingedToposEquivalence` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.fppf_affineToBigRingedToposEquivalence`: **Stacks 0GZU** for the five
  topologies of the source — the affine-to-big morphism of ringed topoi has an equivalence as
  its inverse image and an isomorphism as its structure morphism.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.forget_map_affineToBigRingedToposHom_fSharpInverseImage`: the structure
  morphism is the identity on underlying sheaves of sets.

## Implementation notes

The structure sheaf of the affine subsite is taken to be the literal restriction of the big
structure sheaf rather than a sheaf manufactured by inverse image; the comparison of the two,
`Stacks.Sheaves.ToposPullback.restrictionCommRingIso`, is then the structure morphism. It is an
isomorphism on the nose and the identity on sections, so no hypothesis beyond the dense-subsite
input is needed. `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.affineToFull` (Stacks 0H0C) is the analogue for
categories fibred in groupoids.

The universes of the objects and of the morphisms of the two sites are independent of the
universe of the sections of the sheaves. The right Kan extension used by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsDenseSubsite.sheafEquiv` exists without extra limit hypotheses once
the sections live in a universe containing both sites, so `uliftRingSheaf` first raises the
rings of global functions into such a universe; the five statements below use sections in
`Type (max (u + 1) w)` for an arbitrary `w`. `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.sheafULift`
performs the same lift but only into the universe generated by the site itself, which is not
enough to keep `w` free, so the lift is repeated here on top of the shared lifting functor
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.commRingCatULift`.

Each of the five dense-subsite inputs is an instance (the fppf one,
`AlgebraicGeometry.bigAffineFppfInclusion_isDenseSubsite`, is in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.Fppf.Equivalence`).

